              for its inputs (MCP spec). Nothing more exotic than that. Pocock's
              line keeps it off the Skills ring: "a tool is what the agent calls;
              a skill is instructions it reads."

              Polarion and Jira ARE wired up through MCP here today — that is why
              they are on the wall. Say so if asked; do not oversell it into a
              programme.

              HELD ANSWER — "the Environment ring says my codebase, but you just
              said Polarion." Correct, and deliberate: the ring says codebase
              because that is where your leverage is. MCP widens what the loop can
              reach; it does not move the leverage. (Knowingly left as-is by Atil
              on #36 — do not "fix" the ring's wording without reopening it.)

              HELD ANSWER — "isn't MCP just an API gateway?" No, and that is why
              the word is not on screen. There is no central point (the host opens
              one client per server) and the protocol enforces no policy, no rate
              limit and no central audit. "MCP gateway" is a separate product
              category people sell precisely because MCP alone does not do it.
              What IS true: the server holds the credential and the model never
              sees it.

              COST, spoken not shown — chapter 3 bills this properly. Tool
              definitions sit in the context window on every request, whether you
              call them or not; roughly 55,000 tokens across a five-server setup.
              Say "as deployed today, by default", never "inherently" —
              defer_loading exists and most clients simply don't use it.

              SPEC CHURN, if someone quotes a 2025 article: MCP changed a lot by
              2026 — Sampling and Logging deprecated, sessions gone, the protocol
              now stateless request/response. Don't argue the details, just say
              the 2025 write-ups are a version behind.
            </aside>
